1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,915
So what is deep learning, really, underneath all the excitement?

2
00:00:09,915 --> 00:00:16,300
The definition this course runs on is simulated analog circuits, which just means circuits with knobs on them.

3
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,448
And nobody turns those knobs by hand.

4
00:00:18,448 --> 00:00:22,866
An optimization algorithm sets them, and it's driven by training data.

5
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:27,065
The whole point is that the circuit captures a pattern in its training data.

6
00:00:27,065 --> 00:00:32,866
So when new data arrives, the same circuit still gives a useful answer.

7
00:00:32,866 --> 00:00:36,369
Let's start small, with Rosenblatt's adaptive neuron.

8
00:00:36,369 --> 00:00:40,731
Each input passes through a knob, called a weight, that scales it.

9
00:00:40,731 --> 00:00:43,705
Then we add everything up, along with a bias.

10
00:00:43,705 --> 00:00:49,653
Feed in two and one, and we get one point six, minus zero point five, plus zero point two,

11
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:52,066
which comes to one point three.

12
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:55,151
Then a threshold turns that number into an answer.

13
00:00:55,151 --> 00:00:57,866
It's positive, so the circuit says plus one.

14
00:00:57,866 --> 00:00:59,964
Now flip the sign of both weights.

15
00:00:59,964 --> 00:01:08,633
With the same wires and the same input, the circuit says minus one instead, so the knobs really are the program.

16
00:01:08,633 --> 00:01:10,779
So where does the word deep come in?

17
00:01:10,779 --> 00:01:13,223
It comes from chaining elements together,

18
00:01:13,223 --> 00:01:18,469
so that the signal passes through several of them in a row before it reaches the output.

19
00:01:18,469 --> 00:01:27,166
Each element is still just multiply, add, and take a max, which is the same pattern as before, repeated in sequence.

20
00:01:27,166 --> 00:01:30,608
If nobody sets the knobs by hand, then the data has to do it.

21
00:01:30,608 --> 00:01:35,009
Here are twenty labeled points, and a line drawn from a random starting guess.

22
00:01:35,009 --> 00:01:40,425
The line is where the weighted sum hits zero, and every point on the wrong side gets a red ring.

23
00:01:40,425 --> 00:01:43,900
Right now that's ten mistakes.

24
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,617
The fix is almost too simple.

25
00:01:45,617 --> 00:01:51,362
We grab one mistake, add its label times its input to the weights, and add its label to the bias.

26
00:01:51,362 --> 00:01:56,733
The line swings toward that point, and the mistakes drop from ten to four.

27
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:58,796
Do it again and we're down to two.

28
00:01:58,796 --> 00:02:03,711
But the next update jumps back up to six, so a single step can make things worse.

29
00:02:03,711 --> 00:02:07,715
Keep going anyway, and after four updates there are zero mistakes.

30
00:02:07,715 --> 00:02:12,333
Nobody chose those knob settings, the data did.

31
00:02:12,333 --> 00:02:15,185
But we never really cared about those twenty points.

32
00:02:15,185 --> 00:02:20,945
So let's draw a hundred fresh points from the same source, and the circuit gets ninety-two of them right.

33
00:02:20,945 --> 00:02:28,166
The misses all hug the boundary, because the learned line is close to the real rule, just not exactly on it.

34
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:30,383
Now change the rule behind the labels.

35
00:02:30,383 --> 00:02:37,150
The same circuit gets only fifty-three right, which is a coin flip, because it learned one pattern and not this one.

36
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:39,950
That's why the definition says relevant pattern.

37
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:44,466
Learning only pays off if the new data follows the same rule.

